#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167731} :activity/id #uuid "bda11fb3-0944-54f5-924b-435fbfe6a4d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049898 :topic/id #uuid "1ca243ae-9fa5-5ab8-84a1-6ee2823aded8" :topic/math-academy-id 3176} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166677 :question/id #uuid "d6a54cb2-ea48-57b1-bf2d-7aac09915159" :question/math-academy-id "e-8595"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395658}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"} :step/id #uuid "a6b5756d-4634-5ea9-aec5-a8beb78d3e41" :step/math-academy-id 31928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166678 :question/id #uuid "b2a775f5-4a27-5cba-931f-2a45db305152" :question/math-academy-id "e-8596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395679}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"} :step/id #uuid "171b5dbe-8666-5a5c-8f90-fe3b44d099a2" :step/math-academy-id 31929 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166679 :question/id #uuid "e805dcee-247b-5aa3-9856-f7bd48bd1a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-8597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395693}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "45a7a61e-103e-532f-aa53-42180b78442d" :step/math-academy-id 31930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168115 :tutorial/content "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\right\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nAt [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}6,[/math] the point on the curve, unit tangent, and principal normal are, respectively, given by\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\left\\langle 1, -\\sqrt3, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing the formula to calculate the binormal vector, we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{B}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\t&= \\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\times \\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\, \\begin{array}{|ccc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\left[ \\ \\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf i -\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf j +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf k \\ \\right] \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle (0-\\sqrt3), -(0-(-1)), (-3-1) \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, -4 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the curve [math]\\mathbf{r}(t),[/math] along with its tangent, normal, and binormal vectors at [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.[/math] Notice that [math]\\mathbf{T}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{N}[/math] are both orthogonal to [math]\\mathbf{B}.[/math]\n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N,$ we can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N,$ and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "eeccaac7-95df-5a69-8449-18e90f7ef33a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5544 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ec2925b5-6f20-57e1-ac41-26fc9b56da32" :step/math-academy-id 34413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167676}}] :activity/title "Binormal Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166979}]}
